          "heading": "Unit 1, Lesson 4 \u2014 Ultrafiltration and filtration resistances",
          "content": "# Unit 1, Lesson 4 \u2014 Ultrafiltration and filtration resistances\n\n## Purpose and operating question\n\nMembrane and deposit resistances add in a simple Darcy-type flux model. **Why this matters:** An upstream clarifier sends a variable dissolved-solids stream to a pressure-driven membrane unit. A pressure set point produces acceptable flux at startup but not after several days. Sampling shows no large change in bulk solute concentration. Investigate polarization, surface deposition and pressure loss separately. The correct response is not automatically increased pressure: rising net driving force can exacerbate local precipitation and shorten cleaning intervals. A discriminating test compares normalized permeability after clean-water flushing with the permeability during process operation.\n\n## Physical interpretation\n\nAn ultrafiltration membrane is often run below the pressure that would maximize instantaneous clean-water flux. Proteins, colloids and suspended solids can cause reversible cake resistance or irreversible pore resistance. Resistance-in-series models are useful for diagnostic comparison because they link changes in transmembrane pressure, viscosity and normalized flux. They cannot by themselves identify the mechanism of fouling. Use clean-water tests, controlled hydraulic conditions and mass-balance measurements to discriminate causes.\n\nThe principal mathematical relationship used in this lesson is\n\n`J=DeltaP/[mu(R_m+R_f)]`\n\nThe equation is a compact model rather than an instruction to ignore transport resistance, property dependence or other equipment conditions. Before using it, translate every mathematical term into a measurable quantity. Identify whether the driving force is a local value at an interface, an average over equipment, or a measured inlet-to-outlet difference. Units and operating conditions must accompany any quoted property coefficient.\n\n## Derivation strategy and limitations\n\n**Start from the model:** `J=DeltaP/[mu(R_m+R_f)]`. Associate every ideal parameter with a realistic location in a piece of equipment: a membrane surface, packed bed, crystallizer slurry or filter cake.\n\nThe physical assumptions matter more than a successful algebraic manipulation. If the property varies with temperature or composition, calculate locally or integrate over the changing state rather than using a single value without justification. For time-dependent problems, distinguish a true transient solution from a sequence of pseudo-steady snapshots. For multiphase systems, check that a concentration or composition basis has not silently changed from mole to mass fraction.\n\nFlux models require SI pressure in pascals and are valid only for positive pressures and physically meaningful resistances.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nAssume DeltaP=1.4 bar, viscosity 0.001 Pa s, clean resistance Rm=1.2e12 m^-1, and fouling resistance Rf=0.8e12 m^-1. Determine water velocity then LMH. Compare Rf=0 and Rf=2.4e12 m^-1, keeping the pressure constant. Discuss when an operational cleaning cycle may recover the initial resistance.\n\n**Solution approach:** First list the information provided, and convert all values to consistent units. Substitute only the values that are supported by the statement; identify any additional properties or conditions that would need measurement for a full numerical result. Use the central relation `J=DeltaP/[mu(R_m+R_f)]` as the mathematical starting point, then evaluate a limiting or reference case. The final conclusion should distinguish an explicitly calculated response from a hypothetical plant-level extrapolation. Do not invent the unspecified flow, area, equilibrium or mass-transfer parameters to make a single definitive answer appear possible.\n\n## Engineering decision and diagnostic testing\n\nWrite an equipment-level feed/product/residue table. Do not claim whole-plant recovery from a local equilibrium or point-transport relation. Write an equipment-level feed/product/residue table. Do not claim whole-plant recovery from a local equilibrium or point-transport relation.\n\nA meaningful experiment uses paired samples or simultaneous inlet/outlet measurements at a steady operating condition. Record the sampling interval and the instrument calibration or reference standard. If the observed response changes as throughput rises, test whether the change originates in the material property, the boundary layer, equilibrium constraints or residence-time distribution. When comparing equipment, express the outcome as recovery, product purity, energy or material consumption **on the same basis**.\n\n## Failure mechanisms and how to investigate them\n\n- **Inconsistent basis:** Recalculate using one mass or molar basis and check whether every outflow is physically nonnegative. In a composition balance, sum the species to unity after solving.\n- **A parameter outside its fitted range:** Compare the new operating condition with the measurements that established the coefficient. An apparently precise result may be unreliable under a different feed chemistry.\n- **An unmodeled resistance:** Determine whether external transfer, internal diffusion, hydraulic pressure losses or phase equilibrium changes could constrain the response.\n- **An ambiguous measurement:** Confirm the sample location and timing. An apparent difference in purity may be caused by mixing product samples across a transition.\n- **A neglected waste or utility duty:** Draw the full separation train and account for regenerant, wash solvent, backwash, concentrate or mechanical power as applicable.\n\n## Sensitivity study\n\nRetain the reference case from the worked investigation, then change the principal driving force by +10% while holding the model's other independent parameters fixed. Repeat for the strongest resistance or equilibrium coefficient. If the response is strongly nonlinear, a percentage change in the input does not imply the same percentage change in product. Where the result is dimensionless, verify that it remains within physical bounds. Where the result is a cumulative amount, confirm the time and inventory basis.\n\n## Fully worked numerical demonstration\n\nTransmembrane pressure is 1.4\u00d710\u2075 Pa. Initial total resistance is (1.2+0.8)\u00d710\u00b9\u00b2=2.0\u00d710\u00b9\u00b2 m\u207b\u00b9 and \u03bc=0.001 Pa s. J=1.4\u00d710\u2075/(0.001\u00b72.0\u00d710\u00b9\u00b2)=7.0\u00d710\u207b\u2075 m/s=252 LMH. For clean membrane Rf=0, J=420 LMH. For more severe fouling Rf=2.4\u00d710\u00b9\u00b2, J\u2248116.7 LMH. The predictions are numerically self-consistent; industrial ultrafiltration flux may be constrained by concentration polarization, gel layers or membrane limits before reaching these values.\n\n## Questions for independent work\n\n1. Explain the physical meaning and units of every symbol in `J=DeltaP/[mu(R_m+R_f)]` and identify one property that would need experimental calibration.\n2. Solve the worked investigation with a justified perturbation to its largest uncertain input, then report the direction of the result and a dimensional check.\n3. Describe a realistic counterexample where the relation would fail even though all arithmetic was correctly implemented.\n4. Develop a plant-level material balance that includes both recovered product and rejected or retained material.\n5. Compare the model's predicted effect of increasing the driving force with an actual equipment constraint and state which measurement would reveal the discrepancy.\n\n## Further study\n\nRead the related unit chapter and complete the unit assessment. Use the relevant simulation in the [laboratory directory](../index.html#labs) to investigate a parameter change. Keep the model limitations visible when drawing an engineering conclusion."
