          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Membrane Transport and Pressure-Driven Separations",
          "content": "# Unit 1: assessment and answer guidance\n\nThese questions require a declared basis, explicit assumptions and units; no safety-critical design decision is authorized by classroom examples.\n\n## Topic 1: Solution\u2013diffusion and selective membranes\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `J_i=(P_i/ell)(p_i,f-p_i,p)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Compute two component fluxes for a membrane that has A-permeance 40 GPU and B-permeance 5 GPU. For A, the feed and permeate partial pressures are 300 and 20 kPa. For B they are 200 and 50 kPa. The ideal selectivity is 8, yet the flux ratio is not 8: partial-pressure driving forces differ. Convert GPU to SI before determining molar flux; compare flux rather than claiming a complete module recovery.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Module-level composition and pressure profiles are absent from this local flux calculation; do not use it to guarantee product purity. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q1.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q1.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `J_i=(P_i/ell)(p_i,f-p_i,p)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cModule-level composition and pressure profiles are absent from this local flux calculation; do not use it to guarantee product purity.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 2: Reverse-osmosis osmotic pressure and water flux\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `J_w=L_p(DeltaP-DeltaPi)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Take Lp=2.1 L m^-2 h^-1 bar^-1, hydraulic difference 16 bar, and an assumed osmotic-pressure difference 9 bar. Estimate initial water flux as 14.7 LMH. If concentration polarization raises the osmotic difference to 12 bar, the predicted flux is 8.4 LMH. Compare membrane areas required to produce 24 m3/day using the two flux values, recognizing that a real train has pressure loss and axial variation.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 Model validation:** A van \u2019t Hoff classroom model is not an appropriate design model for concentrated seawater or mixed electrolyte streams. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q2.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q2.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `J_w=L_p(DeltaP-DeltaPi)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cA van \u2019t Hoff classroom model is not an appropriate design model for concentrated seawater or mixed electrolyte streams.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 3: Concentration polarization and limiting flux\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `C_m/C_b=exp(J_v/k)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 Calculation:** At 24 LMH convert the permeate velocity to meters per second. Take k=2.0e-5 m/s and calculate wall-to-bulk enrichment. Repeat at k=0.8e-5 m/s. Explain why the same nominal rejection and bulk feed can have different observed permeability as a result of hydrodynamics.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The exponential film expression is not valid once cake buildup, strong diffusion variation, chemical precipitation or pore blocking dominates. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q3.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q3.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `C_m/C_b=exp(J_v/k)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe exponential film expression is not valid once cake buildup, strong diffusion variation, chemical precipitation or pore blocking dominates.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 4: Ultrafiltration and filtration resistances\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `J=DeltaP/[mu(R_m+R_f)]`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Assume DeltaP=1.4 bar, viscosity 0.001 Pa s, clean resistance Rm=1.2e12 m^-1, and fouling resistance Rf=0.8e12 m^-1. Determine water velocity then LMH. Compare Rf=0 and Rf=2.4e12 m^-1, keeping the pressure constant. Discuss when an operational cleaning cycle may recover the initial resistance.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Flux models require SI pressure in pascals and are valid only for positive pressures and physically meaningful resistances. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q4.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q4.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `J=DeltaP/[mu(R_m+R_f)]`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cFlux models require SI pressure in pascals and are valid only for positive pressures and physically meaningful resistances.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made."
