          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Gas Permeation, Electrodialysis and Advanced Membranes",
          "content": "# Unit 2: engineering project \u2014 Membrane systems and electrically driven separations\n\n## Design brief\n\nA plant uses a membrane cascade to recover valuable solvent vapor while discharging a depleted stream. The first pass appears favorable because the permeate is rich in the desired component. A system audit reveals that compression power and purge losses grow sharply with the recycle ratio. Design comparisons should use total product recovery and energy per unit saleable product, not only membrane selectivity. For ionic brines a separate electrodialysis step may be possible, but charge-equivalent balances and membrane chemical stability must be considered.\n\n## Deliverables and evidence\n\n1. A stream table with all independent feeds, products, residues and recycle flows; define a common mass or molar basis.\n2. A labeled process diagram naming the physical location of the driving force and the location of the main measurement.\n3. A one-page model statement with every coefficient, unit, validity range and inferred rather than measured property.\n4. A nominal calculation and at least four parameter-variation cases, including a counterexample outside the simplifying assumptions.\n5. A safety and environmental note describing rejected material, regenerant, solvent or concentrate management.\n6. A short conclusion that states what the simulation can support, what it cannot, and the next experimental validation needed.\n\n## Technical focus\n\n- **Nanofiltration, selectivity and solute passage** \u2014 employ `R_obs=1-C_p/C_f` only within its assumptions. Ion-specific transport is complex; a constant-rejection balance is a bookkeeping exercise, not a predictive electrodiffusion model.\n- **Gas permeation modules and stage cut** \u2014 employ `theta=F_p/F_f` only within its assumptions. The permeate purity is an assumed module outcome, not a prediction of selectivity alone.\n- **Multistage membranes and recycle balances** \u2014 employ `F z_F=P y_P+R x_R` only within its assumptions. A balance determines feasible streams only after sufficient concentrations or stage performances are specified.\n- **Electrodialysis and electrical work** \u2014 employ `n=eta I t/(z F)` only within its assumptions. Energy per removed mole requires the time-varying stack voltage; charge-only models cannot estimate operating cost.\n\n## Assessment rubric\n\n- Material and energy accounting: 25%.\n- Correct governing equations and units: 25%.\n- Experimental test strategy: 20%.\n- Sensitivity, uncertainty and limits: 20%.\n- Clear engineering communication: 10%.\n\n**Safety boundary:** No classroom model in this volume substitutes for validated pressure-equipment, chemical-handling or environmental-compliance design."
