          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Adsorption Equilibrium, Fixed Beds and Ion Exchange",
          "content": "# Unit 3: assessment and answer guidance\n\nThese questions require a declared basis, explicit assumptions and units; no safety-critical design decision is authorized by classroom examples.\n\n## Topic 1: Adsorption equilibrium and selectivity\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `q_e=q_max K C/(1+K C)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 Calculation:** For qmax=125 mg/g and K=0.08 L/mg, calculate equilibrium loading at C=5, 20 and 100 mg/L. Determine the solute mass held by 1.5 kg of clean adsorbent at C=20 mg/L if every gram reaches the stated equilibrium. Explain why a finite batch may not have enough dissolved solute to reach this loading.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 Model validation:** These coefficients represent a specified adsorbent\u2013solute\u2013temperature combination; do not transfer them to another chemistry without data. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q1.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q1.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `q_e=q_max K C/(1+K C)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThese coefficients represent a specified adsorbent\u2013solute\u2013temperature combination; do not transfer them to another chemistry without data.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 2: Adsorption kinetics and film versus pore transport\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `dq/dt=k_LDF(q_star-q)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 Calculation:** For qstar=48 mg/g, initial loading zero and LDF rate constant 0.035 min^-1, calculate q after 5, 20 and 60 min. Compute the half-time and show why increasing a batch contact time from 60 to 120 min has diminishing returns under this assumption. Identify two process observations that would contradict first-order uptake.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Single-exponential uptake assumes constant equilibrium concentration; coupled batch depletion needs a liquid-phase balance. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q2.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q2.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `dq/dt=k_LDF(q_star-q)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cSingle-exponential uptake assumes constant equilibrium concentration; coupled batch depletion needs a liquid-phase balance.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 3: Fixed-bed breakthrough and regeneration\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `C_out/C_in=1/[1+exp(k(t_50-t))]`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 Calculation:** A measured bed follows a logistic approximation with t50=210 min and steepness k=0.032 min^-1. Determine C/C0 at 150, 210 and 260 min. Find when the outlet reaches 10% of feed concentration; compare that breakthrough time to t50. Discuss whether increasing the bed by 20% must increase t50 by exactly 20%.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 Model validation:** A logistic front does not resolve multicomponent displacement, irreversible fouling or changing adsorption temperature. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q3.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q3.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `C_out/C_in=1/[1+exp(k(t_50-t))]`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cA logistic front does not resolve multicomponent displacement, irreversible fouling or changing adsorption temperature.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 4: Ion-exchange capacity and exhaustion\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `Q_use=m_resin q_eq eta_use`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 Calculation:** A bed contains 45 kg dry resin with rated capacity 3.2 eq/kg and 65% usable fraction. Calculate working capacity in equivalents. For inlet total hardness 4 meq/L and a desired treated-water volume, estimate exhaustion throughput in liters. Repeat at 6 meq/L; quantify the resulting reduction in service time at fixed flow.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The model ignores mass-transfer zone leakage and competitive species; verify bed performance experimentally. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q4.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q4.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `Q_use=m_resin q_eq eta_use`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe model ignores mass-transfer zone leakage and competitive species; verify bed performance experimentally.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made."
