          "secNumber": "4.9",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Chromatography and Preparative Resolution",
          "content": "# Unit 4: assessment and answer guidance\n\nThese questions require a declared basis, explicit assumptions and units; no safety-critical design decision is authorized by classroom examples.\n\n## Topic 1: Retention, selectivity and capacity factor\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `k_prime=(t_R-t_0)/t_0`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 Calculation:** An unretained tracer emerges at 1.3 min and solutes A and B at 4.7 and 5.4 min. Compute each retention factor and the selectivity alpha=kB/kA for the more strongly retained component. Describe why changing flow rate can alter all three times without changing the equilibrium selectivity.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Retention factors are meaningful only for a specified mobile phase, column, temperature and measurement convention. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q1.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q1.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `k_prime=(t_R-t_0)/t_0`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cRetention factors are meaningful only for a specified mobile phase, column, temperature and measurement convention.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 2: Plate theory and band broadening\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `H=A+B/u+C u`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Given A=0.014 cm, B=0.009 cm^2/s and C=0.055 s, calculate H at velocities 0.2, 0.4 and 0.8 cm/s. Differentiate H with respect to u to obtain u_opt=sqrt(B/C), and determine the plate count for a 30 cm column at the optimum. Explain which experimental measurements would test this model.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The equation is a semi-empirical teaching correlation; values differ with solute and mobile phase. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q2.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q2.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `H=A+B/u+C u`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe equation is a semi-empirical teaching correlation; values differ with solute and mobile phase.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 3: Peak resolution, detection and fraction cuts\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `R_s=2(t_R2-t_R1)/(w_1+w_2)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Two peaks have baseline retention times 8.0 and 9.4 min and widths 0.9 and 1.1 min. Compute Rs. Compare a second operating condition with retention times 8.3 and 9.8 min but wider peaks 1.2 and 1.4 min. Use Gaussian curves to estimate visually which fraction window loses more target solute.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Gaussian peaks omit tailing from adsorption heterogeneity and overloaded injections. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q3.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q3.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `R_s=2(t_R2-t_R1)/(w_1+w_2)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cGaussian peaks omit tailing from adsorption heterogeneity and overloaded injections.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 4: Gradient elution, scaling and preparative recovery\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `ln k(phi)=ln k_0-S phi`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Consider k0=20, S=4 and solvent fraction phi=0.35. Estimate retention factor and tR for a void time of 1.5 min using a locally constant-composition approximation. Repeat at phi=0.50. Explain why these results cannot be used directly to forecast a continuously programmed gradient.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Only the local solvent-strength correlation is represented; time-dependent gradient propagation is beyond this model. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q4.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q4.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `ln k(phi)=ln k_0-S phi`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cOnly the local solvent-strength correlation is represented; time-dependent gradient propagation is beyond this model.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made."
