          "content": "# Unit 5: assessment and answer guidance\n\nThese questions require a declared basis, explicit assumptions and units; no safety-critical design decision is authorized by classroom examples.\n\n## Topic 1: Supersaturation, solubility and metastable zones\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `S=C/C_sat(T)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 Calculation:** A hypothetical solute has solubility 180 g/kg solvent at 50 C and 105 g/kg at 25 C. A batch containing 155 g/kg is cooled from 50 to 25 C. Calculate the initial and final S and explain when seeded crystallization becomes feasible. How does seed surface area influence induction time and crystal size?\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Linearized solubility curves are illustrations only; real systems require measured equilibrium data. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q1.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q1.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `S=C/C_sat(T)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cLinearized solubility curves are illustrations only; real systems require measured equilibrium data.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 2: Primary nucleation, secondary nucleation and growth\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `G=k_g(DeltaC)^g`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Assume growth velocity is 0.06 mm/h at a supersaturation driving force of 10 g/kg, with first-order dependence. Estimate growth velocity at 6 and 15 g/kg. For a constant 0.06 mm/h and six-hour batch, calculate ideal change in crystal length. Discuss why a real crystal population does not all grow by the same amount.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The example is for a fictitious compound with illustrative kinetics, not a general law for all crystals. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q2.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q2.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `G=k_g(DeltaC)^g`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe example is for a fictitious compound with illustrative kinetics, not a general law for all crystals.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 3: Batch cooling and crystal mass yield\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `m_crystal=m_solvent(C_initial-C_final)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Charge a crystallizer with 800 kg solvent and 190 g solute per kg solvent at a warm condition. Cool to a condition where equilibrium solubility is 118 g/kg. Estimate equilibrium mass of dry crystal available from the solubility difference. If only 85% of this mass is recovered and the product filter cake retains 20 kg solution, quantify separate crystal recovery and mother-liquor contamination terms.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The calculation treats the crystallizing species as the only solute and assumes no solvent change. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q3.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q3.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `m_crystal=m_solvent(C_initial-C_final)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe calculation treats the crystallizing species as the only solute and assumes no solvent change.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 4: Population balance and crystal-size distribution\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `n(L)=n_0 exp[-L/(G tau)]`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Take G=0.08 mm/h and mean residence time tau=3 h. Compute the exponential size scale G tau and the ratio n(0.5 mm)/n(0.1 mm). Repeat at tau=5 h. Explain how agglomeration and growth-rate dispersion would change the simple exponential population shape.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 Model validation:** This ideal MSMPR population lacks aggregation, breakage, classification and shape dependence. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q4.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q4.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `n(L)=n_0 exp[-L/(G tau)]`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThis ideal MSMPR population lacks aggregation, breakage, classification and shape dependence.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made."
