          "content": "# Unit 6: assessment and answer guidance\n\nThese questions require a declared basis, explicit assumptions and units; no safety-critical design decision is authorized by classroom examples.\n\n## Topic 1: Cake filtration and membrane-prefilter design\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `t/V=mu alpha C V/(2 A^2 DeltaP)+mu R_m/(A DeltaP)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Using mu=1 mPa s, A=0.5 m2, DeltaP=100 kPa, C=8 kg/m3, alpha=1e11 m/kg and Rm=1e10 m^-1, estimate the time needed for 0.05 m3 filtrate. Calculate the contribution of medium resistance separately from cake resistance. Repeat at twice the filtrate volume to see why average throughput falls.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The basic model assumes incompressible cakes and constant viscosity and solid loading. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q1.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q1.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `t/V=mu alpha C V/(2 A^2 DeltaP)+mu R_m/(A DeltaP)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe basic model assumes incompressible cakes and constant viscosity and solid loading.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 2: Sedimentation, thickening and clarifier loading\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `v_t=(rho_p-rho_f)g d^2/(18 mu)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Compare settling velocities for 20 and 50 micrometer spheres with density 2650 kg/m3 in water of density 1000 kg/m3 and viscosity 1.0 mPa s. Calculate their particle Reynolds numbers from the resulting velocities and check whether the Stokes assumption is self-consistent. Interpret why a very small particle is difficult to remove with gravity alone.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 Model validation:** No hindered settling, turbulence or nonspherical drag correction is included. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q2.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q2.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `v_t=(rho_p-rho_f)g d^2/(18 mu)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cNo hindered settling, turbulence or nonspherical drag correction is included.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 3: Centrifugation and accelerated settling\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `RCF=omega^2 r/g`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 Calculation:** At 3000 rpm and 0.12 m effective radius calculate RCF. Repeat at 4500 rpm; identify the quadratic speed dependence. If the effective radius increases from 0.12 to 0.18 m at constant speed, determine the percentage change. Discuss whether separation capacity must change by the same ratio.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The RCF expression is a force comparison; it is not a particle-cut-size or retention-time design equation. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q3.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q3.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `RCF=omega^2 r/g`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe RCF expression is a force comparison; it is not a particle-cut-size or retention-time design equation.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 4: Fluidization and particle classification\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `DeltaP=(rho_p-rho_f)(1-epsilon_mf)L_mf g`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 Calculation:** For rho_p=2500 kg/m3, rho_f=1.2 kg/m3, voidage=0.43 and incipient bed height 0.8 m, estimate the bed-weight pressure drop. Compare with a measured 12 kPa. Identify possible pressure-tap or bed-property explanations if the discrepancy is large.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The simplified bed pressure balance ignores distributor loss and wall effects. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q4.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q4.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `DeltaP=(rho_p-rho_f)(1-epsilon_mf)L_mf g`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe simplified bed pressure balance ignores distributor loss and wall effects.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made."
