          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 14 \u2014 Solvent-strength retention",
          "content": "# Laboratory 14 \u2014 Solvent-strength retention\n\n**Unit:** 4. **Model boundaries:** Local isocratic approximation, not a dynamically transported gradient model.\n\n## Learning objective\n\nIdentify the physical driving force, calculate the principal response, and compare the effect of two independent inputs. Explain the difference between an equation that fits measurements and an equipment design that accounts for full mass and energy balances.\n\n## Adjustable variables\n\nWeak-solvent k\u2080, Solvent-strength coefficient S, Strong-solvent fraction, Void time (min).\n\n## Experiment instructions\n\n1. Record the reference-case output, its units and the assumed basis; confirm that the plotted point matches the numerical readout.\n2. Change **Strong-solvent fraction** to its lower and upper tested values. Export the CSV response, inspect monotonicity and identify any warnings or nonphysical outcomes.\n3. Change one of the other parameters while holding the x-axis variable fixed. Explain the response in terms of the governing model and its dominant resistance or equilibrium contribution.\n4. Estimate a practical equipment limitation omitted by this model, and describe a measurement that could reveal it. Do not claim equipment safety, product specification or regulatory compliance from this simulator alone.\n\n## Interpretation and model limits\n\nLocal isocratic approximation, not a dynamically transported gradient model. This is a transparent educational approximation with explicit parameter inputs. It is not a validated design program. The UI may omit temperature changes, feed composition dynamics, axial dispersion, fouling, energy duties or multicomponent coupling depending on the selected phenomenon. Use the experimental log to identify whether those omitted effects would change the conclusion.\n\n## Three questions\n\n1. Which manipulated parameter most strongly changes the displayed response over the chosen operating range, and why?\n2. Where can the response be nonphysical or the model cease to represent the real process? State a specific boundary.\n3. How would you validate one point of the predicted curve using laboratory or vendor measurements?"
