          "id": "sec-7-9",
          "secNumber": "7.9",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Drying, Leaching and Complex Solid-Liquid Contacting",
          "content": "# Unit 7: assessment and answer guidance\n\nThese questions require a declared basis, explicit assumptions and units; no safety-critical design decision is authorized by classroom examples.\n\n## Topic 1: Psychrometrics and evaporation constraints\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `m_evap=m_dry(X_i-X_f)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Dry 200 kg dry solids from X=0.35 to X=0.08 kg water/kg dry solid. Calculate water removed and the minimum latent heat at an illustrative 2300 kJ/kg. Estimate the ideal air requirement if inlet and outlet humidity ratios differ by 0.012 kg water/kg dry air. Explain why these ideal quantities do not include heat losses and heating of solids.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 Model validation:** Do not equate an ideal minimum vaporization energy with dryer fuel requirement. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q1.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q1.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `m_evap=m_dry(X_i-X_f)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cDo not equate an ideal minimum vaporization energy with dryer fuel requirement.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 2: Liquid\u2013solid leaching and stage efficiency\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `E=1-exp(-k t)`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 Calculation:** A simple batch-leach model has extraction constant 0.08 min^-1. Estimate the fraction of extractable solute recovered after 10, 20 and 40 min. If feed contains 30 kg potentially extractable solute and only 80% is accessible, distinguish recovered mass from the mathematical extraction fraction.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 Model validation:** No equilibrium partition is modeled; the exponential applies only to a clearly defined extractable pool. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q2.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q2.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `E=1-exp(-k t)`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cNo equilibrium partition is modeled; the exponential applies only to a clearly defined extractable pool.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 3: Process integration and hybrid separation selection\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `W_specific=W_total/m_product`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Compare train A with 75% recovery at 2.5 kWh/m3 feed and train B with 88% recovery at 3.1 kWh/m3 feed. Calculate energy per m3 product, not per m3 feed. If B needs chemical cleaning more frequently, identify additional data required for a lifecycle comparison. Describe the direction of bias caused by ignoring concentrate disposal.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 Model validation:** The comparison does not estimate full lifecycle energy, capital cost or effluent compliance. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q3.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q3.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `W_specific=W_total/m_product`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cThe comparison does not estimate full lifecycle energy, capital cost or effluent compliance.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made.\n\n## Topic 4: Scale-up, uncertainty and process safety\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 Interpretation:** Starting from `sigma_f^2 ~ sum[(partial f/partial x_i)^2 sigma_i^2]`, identify the independent driving force and the dependent response. Give their dimensions and explain which parameter must be measured.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 Calculation:** Estimate product mass uncertainty when feed is 100\u00b11 kg/h and recovery 0.85\u00b10.02, assuming independent uncertainties. Apply first-order propagation to mproduct=F*recovery. Explain why uncertainty in recovery can dominate, and why statistically correlated feed and recovery estimates change the result.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 Model validation:** First-order propagation assumes small errors near the operating point and a valid local linearization. Explain an experimental plan to identify whether this limitation is important at the proposed operating point.\n\n**Q4.4 \u2014 Decision:** Compare a higher-throughput operating condition with a lower-throughput but more selective or stable condition. Identify the missing measurements needed to rank them for a plant.\n\n**Q4.5 \u2014 Fault diagnosis:** Suppose an observed result deviates by 25% from the relation. Distinguish data-unit mistakes, instrument bias, a boundary condition change and genuinely invalid model assumptions.\n\n**Answer-check framework:** A correct solution cites `sigma_f^2 ~ sum[(partial f/partial x_i)^2 sigma_i^2]`, uses consistent concentration or mass units, explicitly honors the limit (\u201cFirst-order propagation assumes small errors near the operating point and a valid local linearization.\u201d), and reports a physically sensible direction of change. Numerical calculations must show substitutions and a final unit. Alternative answers can be valid when a different, clearly documented operating assumption is made."
