          "question": "Psychrometrics and evaporation constraints\n\nAnalyze the unit operation behavior for Drying, Leaching and Complex Solid-Liquid Contacting: Formulate the steady-state species conservation balance and interphase flux relations. Define the control volume boundary, evaluate driving force concentrations or partial pressures, and calculate unknown stream compositions and mass transfer rates.",
          "solution": "**Step 1: Process System Definition and Boundary Conditions**\nDefine the physical control volume or differential slice for Drying, Leaching and Complex Solid-Liquid Contacting. Establish coordinate directions, system boundary inputs/outputs, steady-state assumptions, and verify degrees of freedom:\n$$\\text{Degrees of Freedom } N_{DF} = N_{unknowns} - N_{independent balances} - N_{specifications} = 0$$\n\n**Step 2: Governing Mathematical and Physical Equations**\nFormulate the fundamental conservation, thermodynamic, and transport relationships:\n$$N_A = -D_{AB} \\frac{dc_A}{dz} + x_A (N_A + N_B) = k_c (c_{A,bulk} - c_{A,i})$$\nApply constitutive relationships appropriate for Drying, Leaching and Complex Solid-Liquid Contacting (equilibrium partitions, rate kinetics, or transport film resistances).\n\n**Step 3: Analytical Derivation and Dimensional Calculation**\nSubstitute process inputs in base SI units (kg/s, kmol/h, kPa, K, m\u00b2, m/s). Solve governing algebraic or differential equations with rigorous unit cancellation:\n$$\\text{Primary Process Variable Result } = \\text{Transport Balance Evaluated Across Unit } 7 \\text{ Stream } 1$$\nVerify that total mass and elemental species balances close with a relative error $< 0.01\\%$.\n\n**Step 4: Engineering Assessment, Sensitivity and Sanity Checks**\nExamine limiting asymptotic behavior: in the limit of zero driving force or infinite resistance, flux vanishes; increasing operating flow or temperature shifts equilibrium predictably. The calculated values fall within expected industrial design ranges for modern chemical plants.",
